=== layout: section

# Chapter 17B: Genome Editing

=== layout: one-panel

# Genome Editing Is A Design Problem

@body

- What molecular change is needed: DNA sequence, RNA message, or gene activity?
- Which cells must receive the treatment, and how will we measure both benefit and harm?
- The editor is only one part of a therapy. Delivery and DNA repair determine the outcome.

=== layout: subsection

# 13. Editing vs. Therapy

=== layout: two-panels

# Questions: Editing vs. Therapy

@left

- What does editing change at the DNA level?
- What can gene therapy change without changing DNA letters?
- Which goal fits a precise mutation correction, a missing protein, or lowering an overactive protein?

@right

![Gene-editing and gene-therapy comparison](assets/set5_genome_editing/editing_vs_therapy_2025.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 13. Two Ways To Change Biology

@left

- Gene therapy uses genetic material or genetically modified cells to treat or prevent disease.
- It can add a working gene, supply RNA, reduce harmful RNA, or alter gene activity.
- Genome editing is one subset: it changes DNA at a chosen genomic site.

@right

![gene-editing overview](assets/set5_genome_editing/gene_editing_legacy.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 13. Match The Tool To The Job

@left

- *Gene addition or RNA treatment:* a useful product is missing; native DNA can remain unchanged.
- *Genome editing:* a specific DNA change is the therapeutic goal, such as disrupting a harmful sequence.
- Neither label guarantees permanence, one dose, or clinical benefit.

@right

![Editing and gene-addition comparison](assets/set5_genome_editing/editing_vs_therapy_detail_2025.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# 13. Compare Benefit and Risk

@body

- Editing can create unintended DNA changes, large repair products, or harmful consequences of the intended change.
- Gene delivery can miss the needed cells, express too much or too little product, or provoke immunity.
- Ask: *which cells changed, how many changed, for how long, and what did that change do?*

=== layout: subsection

# 14. Zinc Finger Nucleases

=== layout: one-panel

# Questions: Zinc Finger Nucleases

@body

- What are the DNA-binding zinc-finger array and the FokI cutting domain?
- Why are ZFNs usually used as a pair on opposite DNA strands?
- When would error-prone end joining versus template-directed repair be useful?

=== layout: two-panels

# 14. ZFN: Recognition Plus Cutting

@left

- A zinc finger is a protein module that recognizes a short DNA sequence.
- Several fingers form an engineered DNA-binding array.
- The array is fused to FokI, a nuclease that cuts efficiently only when paired with another FokI domain.

@right

![ZFN teaching figure](assets/set5_genome_editing/zfn_2025.jpg)

=== layout: one-panel

# 14. A Paired Cut Improves Specificity

@body

- Left and right ZFNs bind neighboring DNA half-sites; FokI dimerization cuts the spacer between them.
- Requiring two nearby bindings can reduce accidental cutting.
- Each new target still requires protein design and testing in the relevant cells.

=== layout: one-panel

# 14. Read The Repair Outcome

@body

- *End joining* often makes small insertions or deletions and can disrupt a gene.
- *Template-directed repair* can copy a supplied sequence, but competes with end joining.
- Sequence the target and plausible near-matches, then test the edited cells' protein and function.

=== layout: subsection

# 15. TALENs

=== layout: one-panel

# Questions: TALENs

@body

- How do TALE repeats recognize DNA one base at a time?
- Why do TALENs also use paired FokI nuclease domains?
- Compare reprogramming a TALEN protein with changing a CRISPR guide RNA.

=== layout: two-panels

# 15. TALEN Architecture

@left

- TALE repeats come from bacterial proteins that bind plant DNA.
- Each repeat contributes base preference; a string of repeats recognizes a DNA sequence.
- Two TALENs bind flanking sites and bring FokI domains together to cut.

@right

![ZFN and TALEN recognition comparison](assets/set5_genome_editing/zfn_talen_comparison_2025.jpg)

=== layout: one-panel

# 15. TALENs: Strengths And Constraints

@body

- TALENs can target sites inconvenient for a particular CRISPR PAM requirement.
- Their DNA-binding proteins are large, which can complicate packaging and delivery.
- Like ZFNs, they require building and validating protein pairs rather than simply changing a guide sequence.

=== layout: one-panel

# 15. ZFN, TALEN, Or CRISPR?

@body

- *ZFNs:* compact engineered protein recognition; target design can be demanding.
- *TALENs:* modular protein recognition; flexible target choice but large cargo.
- *CRISPR:* guide-RNA recognition makes retargeting fast; PAM, delivery, and repair still constrain results.
- Choose from the target, cargo, cell type, and evidence requirements -- not popularity.

=== layout: subsection

# 16. CRISPR-Cas9: Advantages And Limits

=== layout: two-panels

# Questions: CRISPR-Cas9

@left

- What are Cas9, the guide RNA, and a PAM?
- Why does Cas9 cut DNA but not itself decide the final edit?
- How do RNP, mRNA, and DNA delivery change editor duration?

@right

![Cas9 guide and target](assets/set5_instructor/crispr_cas9_targeting.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 16. From Defense to Editing

@left

- Bacterial CRISPR-associated systems use stored sequence information to recognize invading genetic material.
- A guide RNA provides sequence matching; a Cas nuclease provides the cut.
- Biotechnology repurposes this logic to target a compatible DNA sequence.

@right

![bacterial-defense CRISPR diagram](assets/set5_genome_editing/crispr_bacterial_defense_legacy.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 16. Guide, PAM, Cut, Repair

@left

- Guide RNA base-pairs with a matching DNA target.
- Cas9 also needs a nearby PAM; not every sequence is equally targetable by one Cas system.
- The cell's repair pathways create the observed edit after the cut.

@right

![Guide RNA and Cas9 targeting a DNA sequence](assets/set5_instructor/crispr_cas9_targeting.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 16. Why Fast Retargeting Matters

@left

- Retargeting CRISPR generally means redesigning a guide RNA rather than a whole DNA-binding protein.
- This enables systematic testing and multiplexed targeting.
- Fast design does not remove the need to test repair outcomes and cell response.

@right

![CRISPR mechanism illustration](assets/set5_genome_editing/crispr_mechanism_2025.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# 16. Important Limits

@body

- PAM requirements limit targets for a particular enzyme.
- Similar sequences can be cut; prolonged activity gives more chances for unwanted cutting.
- A DNA break can yield indels, larger deletions, or rearrangements, not just a desired repair.
- Delivery, immune response, and unequal editing across cell types remain practical limits.

=== layout: two-panels

# 16. A Historical Recognition

@left

- The 2020 Nobel Prize in Chemistry recognized Emmanuelle Charpentier and Jennifer A. Doudna for development of a genome-editing method.
- The scientific history includes many contributors to CRISPR biology and application.

@right

![Nobel 2020 figure](assets/set5_genome_editing/nobel_2020_legacy.png)

@citation

Nobel Prize, 2020: https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/chemistry/2020/summary/

=== layout: one-panel

# 16. Historical CRISPR Patent Dispute

@body

- Foundational CRISPR work led to overlapping patent claims and long-running priority disputes.
- The historical teaching question: how did bacterial-system evidence relate to claims for editing eukaryotic cells?
- Do not treat an old slide's litigation status as current legal advice. Patent scope is separate from whether an experiment works.

=== layout: subsection

# 17. Delivery For Genome Editing

=== layout: one-panel

# Questions: Delivery

@body

- Should the editor arrive as DNA, messenger RNA, or protein-guide complex?
- Is local delivery enough, or must it travel systemically?
- Which carrier reaches intended cells while limiting exposure elsewhere?

=== layout: one-panel

# 17. Three Editor Formats

@body

- *DNA:* cells make the editor; expression may persist, so packaging and duration matter.
- *mRNA:* cells make editor protein for a limited time; lipid particles can carry RNA to some tissues.
- *RNP:* Cas protein plus guide acts directly and can be transient; useful for cells edited outside the body.

=== layout: two-panels

# 17. Delivery Is A Biology Problem

@left

- Viral vectors, lipid nanoparticles, electroporation, and local injection reach different cells.
- More exposure can raise editing frequency but broaden opportunities for toxicity or unwanted editing.
- Measure cargo in target cells, edit frequency, tissue distribution, immune response, and clinical function.

@right

![Lipid nanoparticle delivery components](assets/set5_genome_editing/lnp_delivery_2025.jpg)

=== layout: one-panel

# 17. Short Activity Is Often A Feature

@body

- The ideal exposure is long enough to edit enough relevant cells.
- Longer expression can add chances to cut similar sites.
- Delivery format is part of safety design, not a detail chosen after the guide.

=== layout: subsection

# 18. Ex Vivo And In Vivo Editing

=== layout: two-panels

# Questions: Ex Vivo Or In Vivo?

@left

- What are the workflows from collection to reinfusion, or from dose to edited tissue?
- Which cells can be edited and tested before return?
- Which tissues must be reached inside the body?

@right

![ex vivo workflow lead](assets/set5_genome_editing/ex_vivo_2025.jpg)

=== layout: two-panels

# 18. Ex Vivo: Edit, Test, Return

@left

- Collect accessible cells, such as blood stem cells or T cells.
- Deliver editor, then measure editing and cell health before infusion.
- Patients may need conditioning so returned cells can engraft.

@right

![cell-processing visual](assets/set5_genome_editing/ex_vivo_2025.jpg)

=== layout: two-panels

# 18. In Vivo: Reach Cells In Place

@left

- Dose editor directly to a tissue or through the bloodstream.
- This can reach cells that cannot reasonably be removed and returned.
- Infer editing from tissue sampling, biomarkers, imaging, and clinical outcomes.

@right

![in vivo delivery visual](assets/set5_genome_editing/in_vivo_2025.jpg)

=== layout: two-panels

# 18. Compare The Evidence

@left

- *Ex vivo:* characterize the cell product before dosing; collection, conditioning, and engraftment add their own risks.
- *In vivo:* may reach inaccessible cells, but target-cell access and whole-body distribution become central questions.
- A molecular edit is not enough: durable cell function and clinical benefit must be measured.

@right

![Ex vivo and in vivo workflow comparison](assets/set5_genome_editing/ex_vivo_in_vivo_comparison_2025.jpg)

=== layout: subsection

# 19. Off-Target Effects

=== layout: big-image

@image

![On-target and off-target genome editing](assets/set5_genome_editing/off_target_effects_2026.png)

@caption

Topic 19: On-Target and Off-Target Effects in Genome Editing

=== layout: one-panel

# Questions: Off-Target Effects

@body

- What makes an unintended site different from the intended target?
- Which tests examine predicted near-matches, and which look more broadly?
- How can dose, editor duration, and guide design change risk?

=== layout: one-panel

# 19. Two Different Safety Questions

@body

- An *off-target edit* is a DNA change at an unintended site.
- An intended *on-target* edit can also have unintended biological consequences.
- A perfectly targeted disruption can still harm a cell if the target has an essential role or the repair outcome is unexpected.

=== layout: one-panel

# 19. Find and Interpret Changes

@body

- Start with the target and likely near-matches; use PCR and sequencing to measure changes there.
- Complement targeted checks with a genome-wide strategy appropriate to editor, cell type, and decision.
- Inspect the intended locus for large deletions or chromosome-scale joins that a short amplicon can miss.

=== layout: one-panel

# 19. Lowering Risk Is A Testable Choice

@body

- Select a guide with fewer risky near-matches; use a high-specificity editor when appropriate.
- Limit editor dose and duration, for example with transient RNP delivery.
- Test clinically relevant cells, then connect detected changes to cell behavior and plausible clinical consequences.

=== layout: subsection

# 20. CRISPR Implications

=== layout: one-panel

# Questions: CRISPR Implications

@body

- What is a convincing chain from edit to clinical benefit?
- Why choose Cas12, Cas13, base editors, or prime editors instead of Cas9 cutting?
- What evidence should appear before a first-in-human study?

=== layout: two-panels

# 20. Capability Is Not Justification

@left

- A programmable editor can change a target in a laboratory model.
- A treatment must reach correct cells, produce a useful outcome, and have an acceptable risk-benefit balance.
- A heritable change adds separate questions of consent and future risk.

@right

![CRISPR mechanism for evidence discussion](assets/set5_genome_editing/crispr_mechanism_2025.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# 20. A Preclinical Evidence Chain

@body

- Confirm the exact edit and repair products at the target.
- Measure likely and broader unintended changes in relevant cells.
- Show corrected molecular or cellular function, then a meaningful disease-model outcome when available.
- Establish dose, distribution, durability, and monitoring before claiming clinical readiness.

=== layout: one-panel

# 20. Read Headlines Carefully

@body

- Clinical programs use distinct routes: edited blood stem cells returned ex vivo, or editor cargo delivered to organs such as liver in vivo.
- A named technology, trial, or approval is not evidence that every disease or tissue is solved.
- Check the target, delivery route, endpoint, follow-up, and population before generalizing.

=== layout: one-panel

# 20. Original Discussion Videos

@body

- Bozeman Science, *What Is CRISPR?* https://youtu.be/MnYppmstxIs
- Kurzgesagt, *Genetic Engineering Will Change Everything Forever - CRISPR* https://youtu.be/jAhjPd4uNFY
- Ask: which claims are mechanism, which are prediction, and what evidence would test each prediction?

=== layout: subsection

# 21. Zygote Editing

=== layout: two-panels

# Questions: Zygote Editing

@left

- What is a zygote, and how can editor components be introduced?
- Why can early edits become mosaic rather than uniform?
- What changes when an edit could be inherited?

@right

![zygote teaching visual](assets/set5_genome_editing/zygote_2025.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 21. The One-Cell Starting Point

@left

- A zygote is the single cell formed when sperm fertilizes egg.
- Researchers can introduce mRNA or protein-guide complexes by microinjection.
- Editing must occur early and consistently for descendants to share the same result.

@right

![zygote-injection visual](assets/set5_genome_editing/zygote_injection_2025.png)

=== layout: two-panels

# 21. Mosaicism Changes Interpretation

@left

- If cutting and repair occur after the first cell division, cells can acquire different DNA outcomes.
- A small biopsy may not represent every future tissue.
- Testing must examine intended sequence, unwanted edits, repair diversity, and the limits of an early sample.

@right

![Mosaicism after zygote editing](assets/set5_genome_editing/zygote_mosaicism_2025.png)

=== layout: one-panel

# 21. Historical Case: CCR5 Embryo Editing

@body

- In 2018, He Jiankui announced edited embryos involving CCR5, framed as an attempt to alter HIV susceptibility.
- This case invites discussion of uncertainty, informed consent, oversight, heritability, and proposed benefit versus justified intervention.
- The legacy slide's investigation statement belongs to its 2019 context; it is not a current-status claim.

=== layout: subsection

# 22. Somatic Editing In Adults

=== layout: one-panel

# Questions: Somatic Editing

@body

- Which adult tissues are practical targets: liver, eye, or blood cells?
- How does delivery choice match each tissue?
- What outcome shows benefit beyond detecting an edit?

=== layout: two-panels

# 22. Somatic Means Nonheritable By Intent

@left

- Somatic editing changes cells in one person's body; it is not intended to alter eggs, sperm, or descendants.
- Target tissue sets delivery route and outcome measurement.
- Eye dosing, liver-directed delivery, and ex vivo blood-cell editing are different biological problems.

@right

![In vivo delivery as a tissue-access problem](assets/set5_genome_editing/in_vivo_2025.jpg)

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. Measure The Tissue's Job

@body

- *Liver:* disease-protein concentration plus liver safety measurements.
- *Eye:* imaging and functional vision tests alongside local safety examinations.
- *Blood cells:* edit frequency, cell counts, protein measurements, and disease symptoms.
- The endpoint must make biological sense for the tissue.

=== layout: one-panel

# 22. Durability Depends On The Cells

@body

- A long-lived or self-renewing edited population can preserve an effect; rapid turnover can dilute it.
- Redosing may be limited by immune recognition of vector or editor.
- Follow-up includes intended benefit, organ safety checks, and delayed adverse events.

=== layout: subsection

# 23. Base And Prime Editing

=== layout: one-panel

# Questions: Base And Prime Editing

@body

- Which single-base conversions can a base editor make?
- How does a prime editor use a guide with a template to write a planned small change?
- What sequencing result shows the intended product and nearby byproducts?

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. Base Editing: A Chemical Conversion

@body

- Base editors couple targeting to an enzyme that converts one DNA base into another within an editing window.
- They can make particular transitions, such as C-to-T or A-to-G outcomes, without the same double-strand-break strategy as standard Cas9 cutting.
- Nearby editable bases and the window location matter: more than one base can change.

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. Prime Editing: Write A Small Plan

@body

- A prime editor uses a Cas-derived targeting protein and reverse transcriptase.
- Its prime-editing guide RNA identifies the site and carries a template encoding a small desired sequence change.
- It can support selected substitutions, short insertions, or short deletions without a conventional double-strand break.

=== layout: one-panel

# 23. Base or Prime Editing?

@body

- *Base editing:* efficient for compatible single-letter conversions; constrained by chemistry and window.
- *Prime editing:* potentially more versatile for specified small changes; design, delivery, and efficiency remain central constraints.
- Both need target sequencing, byproduct measurement, off-target assessment, and functional readout. Neither is a "fix any mutation" switch.

=== layout: subsection

# 24. Epigenome Editing

=== layout: one-panel

# Questions: Epigenome Editing

@body

- What is dCas9, and why can it target DNA without cutting it?
- How can an attached activator or repressor alter gene expression?
- How would we show RNA, protein, and cell behavior changed while DNA letters did not?

=== layout: one-panel

# 24. Target The Control System

@body

- Catalytically inactive Cas protein, often called dCas9, can still be guided to DNA.
- Fusing dCas9 to an activator, repressor, or chromatin modifier recruits regulatory activity near a promoter or enhancer.
- The intended product is altered gene activity, not a changed DNA sequence.

=== layout: one-panel

# 24. What Must Be Measured?

@body

- Sequence the target before and after to verify DNA letters were not intentionally changed.
- Measure messenger RNA, protein, and relevant cell behavior; a transcriptional shift alone may not matter biologically.
- Ask whether the effect persists, fades after editor removal, or changes as cells divide.

=== layout: one-panel

# 24. A Different Benefit-Risk Tradeoff

@body

- Reversible or temporary regulation may help when permanent DNA change is not wanted.
- A transient signal may be a limitation when durable correction is needed.
- Specificity still matters: guides can bind related sites, and recruited regulators can have broader effects.
